nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2a+5b)\\times c & =(-2a)\\times c+(5b)\\times c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2a)\\times c+(5b)\\times c & =-2(a\\times c)+5(b\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(a\\times c)+5(b\\times c) & =-2\\cdot (i)+5\\cdot (-3i+j) \\\\  & =-2i-15i+5j \\\\  & =-17i+5j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039468/example-14746.json" "file_sha256" "16c517a5f695e9678ca885144f8f7f56d12d58dcea4ec9532fa4e0232985a9aa"} {"question" "e-14747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $2\\mathbf{a} \\times (\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})$, if $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-2 \\mathbf{k}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039468/example-14747.json" "file_sha256" "7e50f687756484f4ada3e6150c5c2140d96a381911fa48022cecaa6e2f1f8fef"} {"question" "e-14747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{a} \\times (\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})=( 2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (\\mathbf{a}) + (2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2a)\\times (a)+(2a)\\times (3b) & =2(a\\times a)+(2\\cdot 3)(a\\times b) \\\\  & =2(a\\times a)+6(a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\times a)+6(a\\times b) & =2(0)+6(-b\\times a) \\\\  & =-6(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(b\\times a) & =-6(i-j-2k) \\\\  & =-6i+6j+12k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039468/example-14747.json" "file_sha256" "7e50f687756484f4ada3e6150c5c2140d96a381911fa48022cecaa6e2f1f8fef"}] "usage" {"q-127858" [] "e-14747" [] "e-14746" []} "basis" 1358 "retractions" [[17592186204133 :question/problem "Find $(7a - 2b) \\cdot b$, if $b \\cdot a = ⟨2,-1, 6⟩$."] [17592186203662 :question/problem "Find $(-2\\mathbf{a}+5\\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c}$, if $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = -3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$."] [17592186203662 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n$(a ± b) \\cdot c|= a \\cdot c ± b \\cdot c; c \\cdot (a ± b)|= c \\cdot a ± c \\cdot b$\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n$(λa) \\cdot b|= λ(a \\cdot b); a \\cdot (λb)|= λ(a \\cdot b)$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-2a + 5b) \\cdot c &= (-2a) \\cdot c + (5b) \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n$(-2a) \\cdot c + (5b) \\cdot c|=-2(a \\cdot c) + 5(b \\cdot c)$\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(a \\cdot c) + 5(b \\cdot c) &= -2 \\cdot (i) + 5 \\cdot (-3i + j) \\\\\n&=-2i - 15i + 5j \\\\\n&=-17i + 5j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203663 :question/problem "Find $2\\mathbf{a} \\times (\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})$, if $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-2 \\mathbf{k}$."] [17592186203663 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n$(a ± b) \\cdot c|= a \\cdot c ± b \\cdot c; c \\cdot (a ± b)|= c \\cdot a ± c \\cdot b$\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n$(λa) \\cdot b|= λ(a \\cdot b); a \\cdot (λb)|= λ(a \\cdot b)$\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n$\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n$a \\cdot b|=-b \\cdot a$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n$2\\mathbf{a} \\times (\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})=(2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (\\mathbf{a}) + (2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3\\mathbf{b})$\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n$(2a) \\cdot (a) + (2a) \\cdot (3b)|= 2(a \\cdot a) + (2 \\cdot 3)(a \\cdot b); = 2(a \\cdot a) + 6(a \\cdot b)$\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}{:}$\n$2(a \\cdot a) + 6(a \\cdot b)|= 2(0) + 6(-b \\cdot a); =-6(b \\cdot a)$\n- Finally, we substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n$-6(b \\cdot a)|=-6(i - j - 2k); =-6i + 6j + 12k$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186199614 17592186